{"body":{"text":["A holon is a small piece that is whole in itself, and part of the field and of any whole that holds it. A note is a holon, and so is a program, a type, a component and a mount: the same kind of piece throughout."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00011MW","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A holon is its identity, what it is an instance of, and its body: what it holds. Beside them it may carry a name, by which it is addressed. The identity is given when it is born and never changes; every state it has had is kept as a version, each pointing back at the one before, so how it came to be stays readable."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00011PD","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A type is a schema in the field: the structure a holon's data has. Data is by its nature structure, and types are what let a field hold structure of its own making, declared in the field itself, so a type is a holon too. Programs need data, and types are what they find it by."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00011TQ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, 2026-11-01: type is what allows you to create schemas within the field, what allows for structure, and that is what programs need. How a type is written, and how the store checks a holon against it, is ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M6YBY3G00011XZ"},".*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00011WK","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"leads":{"$ref":"1M6YBY3G0001P4W"},"text":["open"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00011XZ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000014"]}
{"body":{"text":["Each field a type declares is said in prose as well as in structure: what the key means, beside the shape the engine checks. A field is where holons connect, so the fields are the field's ontological connectors, and holons joined at them form a larger ontology, readable by a person, a program or a language model alike."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00011Y2","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, 2026-11-02: the fields of a holon are contact points for prose, so that we can build ontologically upon them; where each holon connects are the ontological connectors, forming larger ontology. That the prose stands beside the checked shape, and models read it, the session's proposal of the same day, which he answered yes.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00011ZZ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A type's fields are to stand beside its identity, out of its body, as its mould, since they say what other holons hold and not what the type itself holds; a cast, but for what comes from it. A holon is then a type by having a mould, and the type of types is let go."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G000121V","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The store as a primitive the author's, 2026-11-01; its shape proposed by the session and agreed by the author, given whole in ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M6YBY3G000127M"},".*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G000126V","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"leads":{"$ref":"1M6YBY3G000138F"},"text":["the store"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G000127M","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000014"]}
{"body":{"text":["A store is one space, and any whole within it can be taken away as a store of its own."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G000127R","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, 2026-11-04, told in ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M6YBY3G000128P"},".*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G000128E","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"leads":{"$ref":"1M6YBY3G00019B8"},"text":["the store"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G000128P","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000014"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, 2026-10-31 and 2026-11-01: a field needs an engine to govern it; carried from OpenLight's engine. How the engine runs a program, in what runtime and how contained, is open.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012BX","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The field is the stores an engine holds, read as one: where holons live together, and what programs and people ask. Holons hold each other as parts and point at each other from their bodies, so from any holon the field can be read upward to what holds it, downward to what it holds, and outward to what points at it."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012D2","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The one field the author's, from the grant; stores read as one carried from OpenLight; how holons relate, the author's, 2026-11-02, told in ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M6YBY3G00012FW"},".*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012F2","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"leads":{"$ref":"1M6YBY3G00015HG"},"text":["the relations"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012FW","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000014"]}
{"body":{"text":["A program is a holon holding code that the engine runs. It declares the types it takes, the engine brings it the data that fits, and what it does, it does through the engine. A run is a program run once, kept as a holon too: what it was given, its result, and the commits its effects became."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012G1","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A program exists only through the engine: it is data until the engine runs it. How a run is carried out is the engine's, and still open."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012HZ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, 2026-11-01: the program only exists through the engine, and how it is executed and run is open. Runs kept as holons his, the same day (\"it does use the field for runs, I would think\"); where they are kept is ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M6YBY3G00012MC"},".*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012JX","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"leads":{"$ref":"1M6YBY3G0001BVH"},"text":["the home"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012MC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000014"]}
{"body":{"text":["A function is not a program. It is pure: evaluated wherever it is used, as in what an interface draws, every time it is drawn, so evaluating it is no event and nothing it gives is kept. A program is run, and a run is an event with a record. Both are first class, and a program cannot be used where a function is."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012MG","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, 2026-11-03: functions are different from programs, and functions are what are used in the UI; we're not renaming program to function, they're still a first class thing; can you use a program in a UI, I don't think you can.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012PN","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A program acts by changing the field, and every change is a commit. The engine carries out the run, but what a run may touch is the field's to say: each place declares which programs may change it, and the engine follows that declaration. So every effect lands where it can be seen, within bounds the field itself holds."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012R3","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, 2026-10-31: the engine governs the execution but is merely governed by the declaration of the field. That each place declares it is the session's reading, as glass's first iteration built it.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012T2","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A completion model is a program, and each tool is a program of its own, separate from it. What is called an AI today is practically a harness: a program that calls a proprietary completion API and runs the tool calls it gets back. In glass the model, the tools and the harness are each a program in the field, taking their context from it and acting through it."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012V9","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The completion model and the tools as separate programs, and AI as practically a harness, the author's, 2026-10-31; the harness as one more program the session's synthesis.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012XJ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["An interface is holons drawn by holons. A component is a holon that declares the type it takes and draws what it is given, made of code or of data; and a mount is a call to one, a component given what it reads, its argument. A component encloses what it is given and opens a space in it, where what it holds is drawn in turn by the components whose types match, so an interface assembles itself level by level."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00012YH","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, 2026-10-31: each component encloses and opens space for new holarchy and new type-matched components therein. The mount is OpenLight's.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G0001316","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M6YBY3G000133J"},": there is no state, no interaction, no navigation; it is something to birth upon the primitives, so it is editable. The one root mount the session's, from OpenLight's.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G000133C","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"leads":{"$ref":"1M6YBY3G0000GBX"},"text":["2026-11-03"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G000133J","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000014"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, 2026-11-01: holons have ids and history, and the store is glass's own, neither git nor SQLite. The shape of ref, commit, index and versions was the session's proposal, agreed by the author that day.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00013AE","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*Reasoned, the session's, 2026-11-01; canonical JSON as [RFC 8785](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8785) defines it, not yet read in full here.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00013E9","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*Proposed by the session and agreed by the author, 2026-11-01, who named the versions the atoms.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00013FP","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*Reasoned, the session's, 2026-11-01.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00013HF","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A commit does not say who made it. A change is made by a program's run, and the run records the commits it made, so the run is what connects a commit to the program behind it. The run is kept in the home of whoever ran it, which is often not the store the commit lands in, so the link runs from the run to the commit, never back."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00013KN","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, 2026-11-02: a commit is added to the program run that made it, so it is already connected, and there may be no need for a by. The shape and the direction of the link reasoned, the session's.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00013NZ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A store is one space, and it may hold many wholes, any of which may one day be taken away as a store of its own; nobody knows in advance where the field will be cut. So a commit is to say which wholes it touched, and a whole's history is read from the commits that touched it alone. A commit that also touched something beyond the whole goes with it whole: what it says of the rest is a few names with nothing behind them, which does no harm, since the holons point at what they are and the commit only says they were changed together. So a commit is never cut in two and a store never needs one writer for the sake of its history, which were OpenLight's answers when a commit was a row inside one store."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00013QC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["*The author's, ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M6YBY3G00013YY"},": commits only exist in relation to what they affect; a whole taken away is shipped with the commits that affected it, and a commit that affected something else pointing to it is something different. And 2026-11-04: a commit is traceable, so you know which spaces it touches; if you ask for the commits of only one space there is really no problem, and none of the problems OpenLight talks about; OpenLight's one writer and broken commits were a compromise, because we didn't think far enough, not to be taken for granted. The per-whole index the session's proposal, left open by him: I've never worked with indexes that much.*"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00013YR","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"leads":{"$ref":"1M6YBY3G0000MCS"},"text":["late on 2026-11-03"]},"id":"1M6YBY3G00013YY","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000014"]}
